{{also|Ming|mīng|míng|mǐng|mìng|Míng}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/mɪŋ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-ming.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|mingen}}, {{m|enm|mengen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|mengan||to mix, combine, unite, associate with, consort, cohabit with, disturb, converse}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*mangijan||to mix, knead}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*menk-||to rumple, knead}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|mengen||to mix, blend, mingle}}, {{cog|de|mengen||to mix}}, {{cog|da|mænge||to rub}}, {{cog|ang|ġemang||mixture, union, troop, crowd, multitude, congregation, assembly, business, cohabitation}}. More at {{l|en|among}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|minge}}, {{l|en|meng}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|past2=meint|past3=ment}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} To [[mix]], [[blend]], [[mingle]].
#* '''1562''', William Turner, ''Baths'':
#*: I founde here and there litle peces of marquesites and stones, '''menged''' with copper, but I could by no sense or wit perceyue, that the bathes had any notable qualitie thereof.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To bring (people, animals etc.) [[together]]; to be [[joined]], in [[marriage]] or [[sexual intercourse]].
#* '''1590''', Edmund Spenser, ''The Faerie Queene'', I.ii:
#*: the old man [...] him brought into a secret part, / Where that false couple were full closely '''ment''' / In wanton lust and lewd embracement [...].
# {{lb|en|UK|dialectal}} To [[produce]] through [[mixing]]; especially, to [[knead]].

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|to mix}} {{l|en|bemingle}}, {{l|en|combine}}, {{l|en|mang}}, {{l|en|meddle}}; see also [[Thesaurus:mix]]
* {{sense|to have sex}} {{l|en|do it}}, {{l|en|get it on}}, {{l|en|meddle}}; see also [[Thesaurus:copulate]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# [[mixture|Mixture]].

===Etymology 2===
Backformation from {{m|en|minging}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|British|slang}} To be unattractive (person or object).
# {{lb|en|British|slang}} To be foul-smelling.

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|minger}}
* {{l|en|minging}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|mingen}}, {{m|enm|mengen}}, {{m|enm|mungen}}, {{m|enm|muneȝen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|myngian}}, {{m|ang|mynegian}}, {{m|ang|ġemynegian||to bring to mind, have in mind}}, from {{m|ang|myne||mind}}, from {{m|ang|ġemunan||to remember}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*munaną||to think}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*men-||to think}}. Merged in Middle English with {{ncog|ang|ġemyndgian||to remember, be mindful, remind, intend, commemorate, mention, exhort, impel, warn, demand payment}}. More at {{l|en|mind}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|minge}}

